<title>USA: U.S. Q4 non-farm productivity rev to +1.1 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Q4 non-farm productivity rev to +1.1 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor estimates of U.S. productivity/costs, seasonally adjusted annual percent changes from prior quarters, on chain weights basis (1992=100):</p>
<p>    -	     - Productivity -	  - - -Output- - -</p>
<p>    -	     Q4-R  Q4-P    Q3	  Q4-R  Q4-P    Q3</p>
<p> NonFarm	   1.1   2.2  Unch	   5.0   5.9   1.9</p>
<p> Manufacturing   4.4   3.8   5.9	   4.5   3.7   5.0</p>
<p> Durable Goods   3.4   2.6   7.0	   3.3   2.3   6.0</p>
<p> NonDurables     5.7   5.0   4.6	   6.2   5.4   3.7</p>
<p> Total Business  1.2   2.4   0.3	   4.8   5.8   2.2</p>
<p>P-Preliminary.</p>
<p>    -	     - - -Hours- - -	Hourly Compensation</p>
<p>    -	     Q4-R  Q4-P    Q3	  Q4-R  Q4-P    Q3</p>
<p> NonFarm	   3.9   3.7   1.9	   3.6   3.6   3.3</p>
<p> Manufacturing   0.1  Unch  -0.9	   2.8   2.9   4.6</p>
<p> Durable Goods  -0.1  -0.3  -0.9	   2.0   2.1   4.6</p>
<p> NonDurables     0.4   0.4  -0.9	   4.2   4.2   4.7</p>
<p> Total Business  3.6   3.4   1.9	   3.7   3.7   3.8</p>
<p>P-Preliminary.  </p>
<p>    -	 Real Hourly Compensation    Unit Labor Costs</p>
<p>    -	     Q4-R  Q4-P    Q3	  Q4-R  Q4-P    Q3</p>
<p> NonFarm	   0.2   0.4   0.7	   2.5   1.4   3.3</p>
<p> Manufacturing  -0.6  -0.3   1.9	  -1.6  -0.9  -1.2</p>
<p> Durable Goods  -1.3  -1.1   1.9	  -1.4  -0.6  -2.2</p>
<p> NonDurables     0.8   1.0   2.0	  -1.5  -0.7   0.1</p>
<p> Total Business  0.4   0.6   1.1	   2.5   1.3   3.4</p>
<p>P-Preliminary.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast fourth quarter non-farm productivity to be revised to a 1.5 percent increase, and unit labor costs to be revised to up 2.1 percent.</p>
<p>Compared to the fourth quarter of 1995, fourth quarter 1996 non-farm productivity rose 0.9 percent, output grew 3.6 percent, hours rose 2.7 percent, hourly compensation increased 3.6 percent, real hourly compensation rose 0.4 percent and unit labor costs increased 2.7 percent.</p>
<p>For all of 1996, the department said non-farm productivity rose 0.7 percent, revised from the previously reported 0.8 percent increase.</p>
